0.1  The surfaces a LabCairn distribution carries
----------------------------------------------------

A LabCairn distribution is not one dependency tree, and a statement about one
tree is not a statement about the distribution. Four surfaces carry
third-party material. Each was measured on its own, and the section that
measures it is named in the last column.

  surface                          items  copyleft / attribution       measured in
  -------------------------------  -----  --------------------------  -----------
  python dependency closure          171  MPL-2.0, LGPL, GPL-3.0       §2.2, §2.3
  shell npm tree                     268  none                       §2.4
  installer-distributed binaries        8  LGPL-2.1                   §2.5
  web npm tree                       266  MPL-2.0, CC-BY-4.0         §2.6

The column names, for each surface, the terms that are not
permissive-and-done. Two kinds appear in it. A copyleft licence is one that
obliges a distributor of a covered file or a covered library. CC-BY-4.0 is
the other kind: it asks for credit, not for source, so it is an attribution
condition rather than a copyleft one, and it is in the column because a
reader who looks there for obligations would otherwise miss it. The column
names licence families, not packages: §2.3 lists the Python closure's in
full, and they are four — MPL-2.0 (certifi, tqdm), an LGPL notice inside
pywin32's bundled adodbapi module, the GPL-3.0-or-later WITH
GCC-exception-3.1 numpy's own licence file carries for its statically linked
OpenBLAS build, and the bundled-material choices inside Pillow and pywin32
(the FreeType Licence *or* GPL version 2 for FreeType, the Scintilla
licence), which carry a non-copyleft option and so are not a GPL obligation
on this distribution.

The column was narrowed before 2026-11-12: this file then covered two
surfaces and said of them, "Nothing in either tree is copyleft", which was
true of the shell's two npm trees and true of nothing else. The installer
binaries and the web tree are the two that were missing, and they are where
this distribution's copyleft actually sits. Nothing here is a statement that
the installer binaries or the web tree are copyleft-free — that is what §2.5
and §2.6 measure, and what §2.5 records as *not yet discharged*.


