2.5.1  What the LGPL-2.1 obligations are, and what has been done
-------------------------------------------------------------------

Two of the eight binaries are LGPL. LGPL-2.1's clauses are quoted here from a
verified copy of the text; the section numbers were measured against that copy
rather than recalled.

**§1 — the licence text and the notices.** For a verbatim copy:

> You may copy and distribute verbatim copies of the Library's complete source
> code as you receive it, in any medium, provided that you conspicuously and
> appropriately publish on each copy an appropriate copyright notice and
> disclaimer of warranty; keep intact all the notices that refer to this
> License and to the absence of any warranty; and **distribute a copy of this
> License along with the Library**.

**§4 — object code and the corresponding source.** The object-code clause
requires the distributor to "accompany it with the complete corresponding
machine-readable source code, which must be distributed under the terms of
Sections 1 and 2 above on a medium customarily used for software interchange",
and its final paragraph adds that where distribution is by offering access to
copy from a designated place, "offering equivalent access to copy the source
code from the same place satisfies the requirement to distribute the source
code".

**§6 — the combined-work path.** Its preamble requires the distributor's own
terms to permit modification for the customer's own use and reverse engineering
for debugging such modifications, and its body requires: "**You must give
prominent notice with each copy of the work that the Library is used in it and
that the Library and its use are covered by this License. You must supply a
copy of this License.**" It then offers a choice of (a) the relink package,
(b) "a suitable shared library mechanism" — a DLL loaded at runtime,
"operating properly with an interface-compatible modified version" — (c) a
written offer valid for at least three years, (d) equivalent access from the
same designated place, and (e) verification that the user already has the
materials.

**The author's own clarification.** The upstream StdUtils release archive
carries `LGPL_CLARIFICATION.txt`, which says that an installer that
"incorporate[s] and use[s] the StdUtils plug-in Library, strictly through the
NSIS plug-in interface", and that contains "verbatim copies of the StdUtils
plug-in Library" is considered "a work that uses the Library" and "do[es] NOT
represent a derivative of the Library", and that such an installer may be
"distribute[d] … under the license of your choice". The same file's
Limitations paragraph says the plug-in DLL itself "will still be distributed
under the terms of the General Public License version 2.1" and adds that "all
restrictions … must still be adhered to". Two things are stated as facts and
not adjudicated here: that paragraph says *General* Public License where the
rest of the file and the plug-in's header say *Lesser* General Public
License, which is an inconsistency inside the author's own document; and the
clarification is the author's interpretation of LGPL-2.1, not the FSF's. Both
of the clarification's preconditions hold for this build — the shipped DLL is a
verbatim upstream copy, and the installer calls it only through the plug-in
interface.

**What the 2026-11-12 build does about it.** Each obligation, and where it
stands:

  * **The licence text and the notice (§1, §6) — DONE.** The installer now
    carries the licence texts for the two LGPL binaries, in the installer's own
    `$PLUGINSDIR\licenses\` directory, beside the plug-ins they cover.

    The mechanism is `shell/nsis/licenses.nsh`, a custom NSIS include that
    `shell/electron-builder.yml` names through electron-builder's `nsis.include`
    option. electron-builder expands the include's `customInit` macro inside the
    installer's `.onInit` function, after `InitPluginsDir`; the macro writes the
    texts to `$PLUGINSDIR\licenses\` with `File /oname=…`. The six texts are
    the ones in `shell/licenses/`: `LGPL-2.1.txt` (the Licence itself, from
    gnu.org), `STDOUTILS-NOTICE.txt` (the copyright notice and the grant
    `StdUtils.dll` carries), `STDOUTILS-LGPL-CLARIFICATION.txt` (the author's
    own notice, which §1 requires be kept intact), `NSIS7Z-LICENSE.txt` (the
    LZMA SDK notice upstream attributes the plug-in to), `7ZIP-LICENSE.txt` (7-Zip's
    own licence text) and `README.txt` (what the directory holds and where each
    text came from).

    Measured on the build of 2026-11-12 — command, exit code and members are
    in §5.4 — the NSIS archive lists **sixteen** members, and six of them are
    the licence texts under `$PLUGINSDIR\licenses\`. The seven plug-in DLLs,
    `modern-wizard.bmp` and `app-64.7z` under `$PLUGINSDIR`, plus the
    uninstaller executable at `$R0`, are the other ten. Extracting the archive
    and hashing the six members gives the sha256s of the repository's six
    files — five of the six are recorded in `shell/licenses/`'s `README.txt`,
    the sixth being that README itself, which cannot record its own hash — so
    the bytes in the archive are the bytes in the repository.

    Two things this does **not** claim. The licence text travels in the
    installer's payload, not in the installed application's directory: the
    plug-ins are extracted to `$PLUGINSDIR` at install time and are not
    installed as files of their own, and the texts sit beside them there. And
    the texts do not change what the two DLLs are: no licence string occurs
    inside `StdUtils.dll` or `nsis7z.dll` themselves (the same grep as before
    returns 0 hits and exit 1 on each), so the notice is a companion of the
    binaries in the archive, not a replacement for anything inside them.

  * **The corresponding source (§4, §6(c)) — PARTLY DONE, NOT YET POINTED
    AT.** Each of the two LGPL binaries has a corresponding source that is
    identified, fetched and measured. What is not done is the offering: §4's
    final paragraph is satisfied by "offering equivalent access to copy the
    source code from the same place" — the same place the binary is obtained
    from — and LabCairn has published nothing, so no place exists yet. The
    owner's decision of 2026-11-12 fixes the channel: the first release carries
    the source archives on its release page, next to the installer. That makes
    each entry below a step a named release performs, and every value in it is
    a byte count or a hash measured on 2026-11-13 (§5.4's step 4b prints the
    fetches), not a value carried forward from 2026-11-12.

    Every binary §2.5 records under an LGPL-2.1 term has a delivery entry: the
    file a release must carry, where that file came from, its byte count and
    its sha256, and the point at which it is offered. `scripts/lgpl_source_release.py`
    prints that checklist out of these entries and, given a directory holding
    the archives, verifies the bytes against the hashes recorded here.

