    **How "corresponding source" is read here, and who decided it.** LGPL-2.1's
    own definition, in the copy this repository carries
    (`shell/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt`), is that for a library "complete source code
    means all the source code for all modules it contains, plus any associated
    interface definition files, plus the scripts used to control compilation and
    installation of the library". Read against that clause, `nsis7z.dll`'s
    corresponding source is the **conjunction** of the two archives above —
    7-Zip's code is modules the Library contains, and `Nsis7z.vcxproj` is the
    script that controls its compilation — which is why this section names two
    artefacts where `StdUtils.dll` needs one.

    **The owner decided this reading on 2026-11-13.** This section recorded it as
    one of two defensible readings and put the choice to the owner; the owner
    chose the wide reading — that 7-Zip's code is modules the Library contains and
    its compilation scripts are included, so the corresponding source is
    `Nsis7z_19.00.7z` **and** `7z1900-src.7z` together. It is recorded here as the
    repository's reading, not as an open question. The narrower reading — that the
    plug-in's own release archive is the corresponding source because it is the
    upstream release the plug-in was taken from — was stated here as the
    alternative and is **not** the reading this repository records; what it would
    change is only the number of artefacts on a release page, since the byte counts
    and hashes are the same either way.

    The smaller choice inside that reading — whether a release carries
    `7z1900-src.7z` or the `lzma1900.7z` archive the plug-in's own build note names,
    since both close the same 174-source list — was settled by the same decision:
    the **full 7-Zip source release** is the artefact §2.5.1 delivers, and §2.5.2
    records why the other archive is named here at all.

    **What is not yet done.** Neither archive is offered from the place the
    binaries are obtained, which is the requirement §4's final paragraph states
    rather than a description of these entries. Until a release page carries
    them the obligation is not discharged, and no written offer under §6(c)
    exists either.

  * **Replaceability (§6(b), and §4's final paragraph for the §4 path) — NOT
    MEASURED.** The route LGPL-2.1 contemplates is a DLL loaded at runtime that
    "operat[es] properly with an interface-compatible modified version". What
    was measured: `StdUtils.dll` is extracted to `$PLUGINSDIR` at install time
    and is not installed as a file of its own, so the replacement route runs
    through the installer archive — a build of StdUtils that keeps the
    `StdUtils::TestParameter` export and its calling convention can be put in
    `$PLUGINSDIR`'s place before the installer is run. What was **not**
    measured: whether an installer carrying such a build still completes the
    installation. No installer with a modified `StdUtils.dll` was built or
    run, so this file records the route and not a tested result. The load is
    narrow — resolving the include tree the generated installer pulls in shows
    it calls **four** of the plug-in's exports, and no other: `TestParameter`
    (seven call sites, through the flag macros `electron-builder` emits
    unconditionally), `ExecShellAsUser` (the installer's start-application
    step), `GetAllParameters` (reading the `/D=` switch) and `GetParentPath`
    (copying the uninstaller) — against the 59 names `StdUtils.dll` exports.
    A replacement therefore has to keep those four working, and "narrow" is
    still not "verified".

